00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.130
Anyway, back to our story.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.500
We're going to talk about listening.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.630
It's a key leadership behavior,

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.720
and Julien's going to
talk about this skill

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.280
from the perspective of his model

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.650
that he created on positive communication.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:16.679
- When you choose to listen
as a leader, you are choosing

00:01:16.679 --> 00:01:19.620
to transcend the perceived
differences that exist

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.720
between you and other people.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.220
Why is listening such an important

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.840
leadership behavior, Alex?

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.880
- Well, listening is an often overlooked

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.580
and usually undervalued
communication skill.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.920
It often doesn't look
like anything's happening,

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.050
but if you want to take
your leadership seriously,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.970
then you have to work on your listening.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.540
Because the higher up leaders
go in any organization,

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.640
the more time they spend listening.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.670
In fact, as we show in our
book, executives spend twice

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.820
as much time listening compared
to the average employee.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.970
And emerging leaders spend
more and more time listening

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:55.500
as they move up the ladder
in any kind of organization.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.840
And people in all walks of life,

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.250
whether they're in an organization

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:03.360
or in their personal life,
love great listeners.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.090
- But there are real
important practical benefits

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.130
to being a great listener.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.740
Leaders who listen well and deeply

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.810
get to know their people better.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.450
They also can be more easily identifying

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.510
the emerging problems that are
taking place on the ground.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.730
They have more input about solutions

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.180
that could be more effective
to solve real problems.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:26.460
They also are really good
if you use listening,

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.490
you can resolve the problems
before they get bigger.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.890
And more importantly, most
of us, we want to avoid

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.470
hard conversations, difficult moments.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.900
If you can listen a
little bit more deeply,

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.610
you can avoid these hard talks

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.550
because you're managing
problems all along the way.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.490
For these reasons, it
doesn't surprise us that

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:47.340
some of the best listeners we've ever met

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.880
are leaders sitting at the top
of the organizational chart.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.160
- So what does good listening look like,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.170
sound like, and feel like?

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.390
Well, in our book, we use
the term deep listening

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.450
to indicate that
listening well goes beyond

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.210
superficial tactics, like fake listening,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.400
to give people the impression

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.200
that you're listening when you're not.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.370
For example, deep listeners
follow three key behaviors.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:13.530
First, deep listeners are fully present

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.170
and completely in the moment.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.360
Deep listeners put their phones away.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.340
They reduce all distractions.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.290
They literally lean their bodies in

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.980
and they listen with their
eyes, not just their ears.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.230
Deep listeners, for
example, are never looking

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.240
over your shoulder to
see who else might be

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.610
a little more interesting in the room.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.800
And a fully present
listener makes you feel

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.170
like you're the most important person

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.150
in the world at that moment.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.030
They're fully present,
and that is a choice.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.640
For example, they say that Elvis Presley

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.100
made you feel like you were
the only person that existed

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.430
in the entire universe while
he was speaking to you.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.900
He made you feel special.

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.020
And if the king of rock
and roll had the time

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.570
to make people feel that way, so can you.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.660
- Deep listeners are also open.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.060
Openness is about welcoming new ideas,

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.920
perspectives, and approaches.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.960
We know that remaining open is difficult,

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.660
but it does not mean
abandoning your point of view.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.230
In fact, openness is all about
maintaining a really fine,

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:17.010
delicate balance between holding
onto your own perspective

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:20.790
and being profoundly open to
the perspective of others.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.460
Openness is typically
difficult for people to develop

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.010
because it involves our ability to accept

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:27.660
and welcome new information,

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.120
which may even contradict
our own thinking.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.790
We interviewed a variety of
leaders for the book that

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:35.647
Alex and I wrote, but
one of them said this,

00:04:35.647 --> 00:04:38.340
"Leaders, in general, are very stubborn.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.010
They believe in something
and they keep on pushing

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.890
whether people agree or disagree."

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:47.347
But he added this important
piece of information, he said,

00:04:47.347 --> 00:04:49.950
"But I have found that listening deeply

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.980
always gives me a new perspective."

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.600
So gaining a fresh perspective,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.360
even if one may not
fully change their view,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.310
is obviously critical for leaders.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.070
So as a leader, we
encourage you to remain open

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.950
to hearing new ideas,
to hearing new thoughts

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.830
and constructs that will
help you innovate, adapt,

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.420
and hopefully thrive.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.960
- The third behavior is that
deep listeners show empathy.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.960
Put simply, empathy is your
ability to put yourself

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.080
in the other person's shoes
to understand their world

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.870
and to feel what they are
feeling in that moment.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.720
Empathy usually involves pitching back

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.640
what you believe you have heard

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:28.710
or asking a few follow-up questions

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:30.840
to make sure you are
understanding the other person's

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.150
point of view accurately.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.760
So I'm gonna give you a few
straightforward examples of

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:38.370
how you might respond in the moment.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.230
We're gonna be really
straightforward here.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.060
When good listeners respond,

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.540
they keep their responses low key.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:47.887
For example, they may say things like,

00:05:47.887 --> 00:05:51.660
"I hear you," "Makes sense,"
"I see what you mean,"

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.790
or even simply, "Hm."

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.490
You'll notice that these
replies are all very short.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:58.200
Great listeners are not eager

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.420
to take their own big talking turn

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:03.630
or to jump in with a
story to match your story.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:06.480
They're entirely focused
on understanding you

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.760
and understanding your world.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.417
As Stephen Covey might say,

00:06:10.417 --> 00:06:13.080
"Great listeners listen to understand.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.810
They don't listen to simply reply."

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:18.450
- Let's get actionable,
here is your challenge.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.070
This is a simple action step,

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.340
but it takes commitment,
and courage on your part

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.800
to execute this challenge.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.060
In the next 24 hours,
strive to make listening

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.750
your dominant goal in any
conversation that you're having.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:35.550
It's gonna be your purpose.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.500
Use the tips that we've
shared in this video

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.470
to listen more effectively
and more deeply.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.050
Remember that it's a part of your job.

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:44.310
Here's a quick summary.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:48.120
Recognize that listening is
a leadership responsibility.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:52.050
And deep listening means that
you become fully present,

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.950
that you become fully open,
and that you become empathetic.

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.870
We know that listening in one-on-one

00:06:57.870 --> 00:06:59.970
conversations is foundational.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.790
And in our book, we also explain
how you can use the power

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:05.790
of listening to build
an extraordinary team

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:08.010
and a great organizational culture,

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:10.380
heart and centered on compassion.

